A||N||18||Jefrysuko||Lightweight reduction gear box||||1||
Z||000000||Jefrysuko||10-30-2001||02:29 PM||||Ok guys,<BR>I am in need a a light weight gear box with somewhere between 2 and 3 reduction.  Why you ask?  Being an engineering student here at the University of Illinois I got involved with SAE (Society of Automotive Engineers) on a MiniBaja competition.  What happens is every school participating in the competition makes a car from scratch and is given a 10hp Briggs to power their car.  Currently we run a CVT (similar to that of a snowmobile or a junior dragster) into double chain reduction. We are looking to rid ourselves of one of those chain reductions and use a gearbox (hopefully with less loss).  All we need is the reduction but something that offers a reverse wouldn't be objected if it was still light weight.  If any of you have some sort of ATV with a gearbox let me know what you know about it, for that might be our best bet.  But any ideas are welcome.  We are even thinking about manufacturing one ourselves.  Thanks for the space Cliff.<p>[This message has been edited by Jefrysuko (edited 10-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||10-30-2001||02:39 PM||||Is your motor horizontal or vertical shaft?<P>A lot of ATVs have the gearbox built into a single case with the engine. You might want to look at some riding lawn mowers. You could get 5 or so forward speeds with reverse in a fairly compact unit. See if a local small engine repair place will let you look through their scrap yard.<P>||||reg||3
Z||000002||Jefrysuko||10-30-2001||04:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>Is your motor horizontal or vertical shaft?<P>A lot of ATVs have the gearbox built into a single case with the engine. You might want to look at some riding lawn mowers. You could get 5 or so forward speeds with reverse in a fairly compact unit. See if a local small engine repair place will let you look through their scrap yard.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Horizontal shaft.<P>Yeah I don't know much about ATV's but some of the guy's on the team think we could run a transfer case from one.  I would think that the riding lawn mower would have to much loss for our application.  We are talking about a 300 lb vehicle doing 40 MPH on a motor-cross course off of only 10 HP.  Even the slightest bit of loss takes a big chunk out of what available power actually reaches the wheels.  Plus we run the CVT which takes care of the transmission part of it.<P>I am looking into Gear Heads right now.  From what I understand these are used in industrial applications with servos when precise control is needed.  What interests me is planetary gears (light and strong) and a single input/output.  What I need to know is if they can handle the conditions we put them through.  I know we only have 10 HP but we get drastic torque spikes from landing that have been known to break shafts strip sprockets and numerous other things.  Anyone use these at work?||||reg||1
Z||000003||falconhulk||10-30-2001||04:50 PM||||hey man they race riding lawn mowers!  They remove the blade and strengthen them up.  They dont have much more the 10 to 12hp and they can get up 80 mph.  Think i am joking?  Do a web search.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jefrysuko||10-30-2001||08:48 PM||||Yeah I know.  I have seen that before also.  The reason they get a little more speed out of those things is that they are able to get rid of the stock governor.  I am not sure what they use for a drivetrain but I would bet it is something better than what was on my old craftsman riding mower.  If I remember right they even have classes, stock, modified etc...<P>We are limited to 3600 RPM so we don't even get to use most of the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||10-31-2001||09:49 AM||||Not trying to be a jerk, but you're an Engineering Student involved with the SAE - design and build your own!  That's the point of these competitions.  Not to see who can source the best parts.<P>Good Luck with it!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000006||Butter||10-31-2001||10:24 AM||||Jefrysuko,<BR>Have you given any thought to a variable pulley belt system for gear reduction?  This could be adjusted to operate close to the peak output of the 3600 rpm engine in order to take advantage of the peak output of the engine at any given speed.  The belt might absorb some of the torque spikes you speak of. ||||reg||3
Z||000007||Jefrysuko||10-31-2001||10:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>Not trying to be a jerk, but you're an Engineering Student involved with the SAE - design and build your own!  That's the point of these competitions.  Not to see who can source the best parts.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes you are right I am an engineering student not a machinist.  We believe that we can design better car by incorporating an aftermarket gear box than if we make our own.  This is simply because we aren't good machinists and we can't hire a professional machinist to do it for us.  We can however buy parts that are readily stocked by a company.  We also are using front spindles and brakes off of an ATV because they are lighter and stronger than we can make.  Also when it comes to brake components would you say that we should make those ourselves?  So you see that we are trying to build the best car that we can within the rules that SAE provides, that is the "point" of the competition.<P>I do appreciate your comments though.  We do try and make as many things ourselves and are one of the teams that are leaders in this.  We however have come to the conclusion that this is a sacrifice we are going to have to make to remain competitive.  There are teams out there that have an unlimited budget and several design teams to make the designs for them every year.  Unfortunately our school does not support us like that and we are left with a dozen engineering students all under classmen and we still manage to build a new car every year with several changes which is something a lot of them don't.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jefrysuko||10-31-2001||10:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Butter:<BR><B>Have you given any thought to a variable pulley belt system for gear reduction?  This could be adjusted to operate close to the peak output of the 3600 rpm engine in order to take advantage of the peak output of the engine at any given speed.  The belt might absorb some of the torque spikes you speak of. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes this is what the CVT is.  I will explain how it works real quick.  Attached to the motor we have a pulley that starts out small and gets larger as the engine speed rises.  There is another pulley that goes from large to small at the other end.  So as you can see we have a lot of reduction out of the CVT at low RPM's and a speed increaser at higher RPM's.  The problem is that we need reduction out of the CVT.  We have thought about trying to make a different CVT but that would be even harder than making a gear box.  Most teams run the same CVT that we do, at least the competitive ones, and the challenge is the get the correct reduction further in the drivetrain with the least amount of loss, and the correct tuning of the CVT of course.<P>You would think that the CVT would offer a little more damping of the torque spikes than they do.  Also we are a little afraid that a CVT that offers more reduction and less increase would be much heavier than the one we currently run.  These thinks are <B>heavy</B>.  If anyone have some suggestions on a different CVT or the like I wouldn't mind hearing about it.  I think we run a Comet right now.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Nashco||10-31-2001||12:50 PM||||Have you thought of using a motorcycle gearbox, and machining a case to suit your needs?  You could find a motorcycle junkyard, and there are TONS of different setups on motorcycles.  Driveshaft, chain driving, belt driven, longitudinal and transverse mounted, lightweight, sturdy, 100+ hp capable, 30 hp capable, etc.  Also, there is an immense range of gear ratios to be had depending on if you're using gears from a dirtbike, cruiser, sportbike, etc.<P>All you need to do is figure out what kind of ratios you want/need, research what kind of bike has those sorts of ratios in general, and then snatch a similar transmission.  Tear into it, use the gears, and draw up everything in CAD.  You can then machine your own case out of lightweight aluminum alloy.  An added benefit to the motorcycle trans is also sequential shift.  You will have to design a reverse mechanism, but this shouldn't be too hard, just need a gear to jump in the middle of things and reverse the output direction.<P>I know that I could make this work if I was limited to this, but there may be better ideas out there.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||Butter||10-31-2001||12:56 PM||||Jefrysuko,<BR>Sounds like a fun competition.  Sorry I didn't know what CVT was in reference too but I knew how they worked.  I also noticed that you mentioned tuning it which is important.<P>Well so much for my first thought.  Let me throw a couple of other ideas your way.  You mentioned weight was important and the use of gears as an option.  Have you given any consideration to using just the proper gears you need from an old gear box and maybe making the housing yourself?  That would be easier than machining the gears and could give you the ability to add a different extra ratio that could be shifted on the fly.<P>Could serpentine belts be used in leiu of the chains and spockets?  This should be less weight and allow for buffing of torque spikes.<P>You may want to surf the net for some of the lawnmower racing sites.  Some of those folks may have some better gearing ideas.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000011||LAMBO||10-31-2001||01:49 PM||||Check into golf carts. Maybe some of the older ones would have a separate forward/reverse gear box. I'm assuming the newer styles would have a similar CVT setup like the snowmobiles or jr. dragsters. Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||10-31-2001||02:55 PM||||Do you really think a gearbox is going to be more efficient than a set of sprockets and a roller chain? I think the added weight of a gearbox would offset any advantage. I like Butter's idea of using belts, more friction but less weight. Maybe a cog belt instead of multi-rib. I have a Comet Torque-a-verter on my 5hp 1/2 scale R/C buggy, those things work great. You have a chain coming off the Comet anyway, so why not just use the chain to get the needed reduction? Can't you get enough reduction from one chain? Otherwise you'll have a chain <B>and</B> a gearbox. I've never seen an ATV or cycle that didn't have the gearbox as an integral part of the engine. My dad has an old ag sprayer with a 10 or 15 hp kohler engine. It's got a gear reduction that bolts directly to the side of the engine. The gearbox has a shaft coming out just below where the motor shaft would be. Not sure what the ratio is or where to get one, but I bet it would work for you.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Jefrysuko||10-31-2001||04:33 PM||||Wow getting into some good stuff now.<P>Nascho, Yes the team has thought about using a motorcycle trans in the past, before my time, but opted for the CVT.  I think the main reason was trying to keep the design as simple as possible.  Also we have a very narrow RPM range 1500-3600 so you would be constantly shifting.  Like I said the teams that are competitive all run the comet CVT.  I will know more about this after we go to competition at the end of the school year.  So far we have never ran a reverse partly because our setup hasn't allowed for it but also due to added weight.<P>Between you and Butler you might be on to something.  If we ran a small transmission off the CVT we could run two gears and then wouldn't be as concerned about loss but wouldn't be shifting constantly either.<P>Butler yes we have run belts and chains but have mostly switched back to chains.  We have used several types of belts, serpentine mainly, and have not had good luck.  Just recently we had coged belt that lost all of it's teeth in just two hours.  This isn't good because we have to run a 3 hour endurance race.  The reason for us trying belts was to dampen the torque spikes which helped but unfortunately the belts take all the punishment that way.<P>Rockcrawl, yes I think we can find a gearbox that will be more efficient.  We might have to lower the oil level and or do a little internal machining but I am sure it can be done.  I am looking at a gearhead that has 92% efficiency and only weighs 8 lb, so that is headed in the right track.  Our comet drives a shaft so we can put a sprocket on the shaft or run it into a gearbox or just about anything we want.  A single chain reduction hasn't worked in the past.  Some teams have tried it but, from what I understand, you will end up striping out a sprocket.  I know exactly what you are talking about with the ag sprayer but unfortunately that won't handle any side loading or the torque we need to put through it.  Those don't even have bearings just brass bushings.  They do work extremely well just not for out application.<P>Another reason we use double chain reduction is because we use chain drive.  Ok so we have the CVT which acts as our transmission.  Off the CVT we have our first reduction (small sprocket into big sprocket if using chain).  The shaft that comes off the first reduction runs through the pivot point of the suspension (we do this because we are running a swing arm with a solid axle, no c/v joints or anything).  On the drive chain we run our second reduction which runs from the pivot point down to the axle.  What we are trying to do is work out that first reduction.  As I have kind of eluded to we have run chain reduction mostly because that offers less loss than a belt.  We tried belts because we have been getting failures due to torque spikes (stripped sprockets, bent shafts, etc...).  The belts helped absorb some of the shock which helped the rest of the drivetrain but in result chews up belts rather quickly.  I have a meeting tonight and will present the two ideas thus far, my gearhead and the idea of part of a motorcycle trans with only a few gears to keep the weight down.<P>Later tonight I will try and find some stuff about the lawnmower racing, since you guy's keep bringing it up   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">, and golf carts.  Thanks for all the input.<P>Edit for stupidity  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Jefrysuko (edited 10-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Nashco||10-31-2001||10:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B>Wow getting into some good stuff now.<P>Nascho, Yes the team has thought about using a motorcycle trans in the past, before my time, but opted for the CVT.  I think the main reason was trying to keep the design as simple as possible.  Also we have a very narrow RPM range 1500-3600 so you would be constantly shifting.  Like I said the teams that are competitive all run the comet CVT.  I will know more about this after we go to competition at the end of the school year.  So far we have never ran a reverse partly because our setup hasn't allowed for it but also due to added weight.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Let us know what you come up with at the meeting.<P>I think you might misunderstand me.  I don't think you should use an entire motorcycle transmission, just a small portion of the gearsets and shifter assembly.  You would need to modify them slightly, perhaps, to be small and still use the shifter assembly.<P>A few different viewpoints:<P>"..we have a very narrow RPM range..so you would need to be constantly shifting."<P>Yes, you do have a very narrow RPM range.  That is perhaps part of the GOOD thing to use motorcycle gears.  You can find exactly the ratios you need if you search around, to try and stay at the peak RPM at any point in time.  You could actually get several gears that were very close ratio, if you manufactured your own mainshaft and countershaft for them to reside on.  As I said, the motorcycle transmissions are sequential shift.  You can feather the clutch and shift a motorcycle transmission VERY quickly, as they were made to work at high RPM.  If you had, say, four speeds and could stay within 300/400 RPM of your peak power range, you may get MORE advantage from a few speeds than you would lose from shifting.<P>I understand the CVT theoretically keeps you at peak power always, but you may realize some significant weight losses with a custom made gearbox.  Also, you have full design capabilities, allowing you to try and arrange the best weight placement and axle location.<P>"Like I said the teams that are competitive all run the comet CVT."<P>To be a front runner, you sometimes have to one-up the competition.  Thinking outside the box is key in this competition, with such limited power.  Perhaps you could think up a better CVT design?  Do you have some info you could share on the comet...website, pics, anything?<P>"So far we have never ran a reverse partly because our setup hasn't allowed for it but also due to added weight."<P>Is reverse necessery or helpful?  Reverse really isn't hard to achieve if you can incorporate a slider mechanism on any one of the gearsets.  Like I said, you only need one gear inserted between any of the output gears to reverse direction, and no syncros are needed for reverse (I assume you'll be stopped before going in).  Just a thought...<P>I'd like to hear/see more on the CVT tranny if you could provide a few links.  Maybe we can be better help if we can have an image and some info to work with.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||AutoXdbdragSE||10-31-2001||10:57 PM||||Hey jeff.. you might have wanted to say what CVT stood for right off.. Constant Velocity Transmission.  Just like what saturn is coming out with.. yes they are now making cars that run just like a snowmobile.. sad sad world isn't it.<P>OH.. my ideas, you probably want these anyway.<P>Use a ring and pinion from a MG midget.. i got one you could use.. i think it's around 3/1 reduction, it also changes direction 90 degrees like you would need<P>use a belt drive motorcycle trans mission off one of the 4 kawasaki 250's i have.. or will have soon.. also around 3/1 only thing you would need is to find a shorter belt.. but i'd give you guys the parts.. well maybe not the pully's.. but the tranny for sure<P>front diff off a 4wheeler.. nuff said<P>well that's all.<P>OH and to the guy that said it was a design competition not a parts sourcing comp.  let me tell you i agree. i'm on the Formula SAE team. we build a mini F1 car out of a 600cc sportbike engine.. it's very much a parts sourcing comp. it's also a engineering competition only not a driving competition..even though 75% of points come from driving skill.. but i won't get into that.. you might find my bitterness about that one.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Jefrysuko||11-01-2001||01:54 AM||||<B> Let us know what you come up with at the meeting.{</B><P>Well not so good at the meeting.  I will get more into that in a minute.<P><B>I think you might misunderstand me.  I don't think you should use an entire motorcycle transmission, just a small portion of the gearsets and shifter assembly.  You would need to modify them slightly, perhaps, to be small and still use the shifter assembly.</B><P>Yeah I understood.  I like the idea of using that between the CVT and the drivechain and only leaving two forward gears from the original trans.<P><B>You can feather the clutch and shift a motorcycle transmission VERY quickly, as they were made to work at high RPM.  If you had, say, four speeds and could stay within 300/400 RPM of your peak power range, you may get MORE advantage from a few speeds than you would lose from shifting.<P>I understand the CVT theoretically keeps you at peak power always, but you may realize some significant weight losses with a custom made gearbox.  Also, you have full design capabilities, allowing you to try and arrange the best weight placement and axle location.</B><P>Yeah I like what you are saying but so far the rest of my team dosn't have any plans on running anything other than the Comet.  This is my first year with them and they have just figured out how to tune the thing so that is what they want to run this year.  Now I will be around for one or two more years.  I have been tasked to model the car in Pro-Engineer, which is a CAD program but you actually model the car in 3-D not just make drawings.  I have plans on making some major improvements over the course of the year and have a new design with an independent rear and less complex drivetrain.  For now I have to work with them or not at all.<P><B>To be a front runner, you sometimes have to one-up the competition.  Thinking outside the box is key in this competition, with such limited power.  Perhaps you could think up a better CVT design?</B><P>Well as you can see my team isn't actually up to thinking outside the box right now.  I have no plans on letting them slow me down though if by this time next year I am not doing something drastically different to that car I would hope someone would slap me.<P><B>Is reverse necessery or helpful?</B><P>Necessary, No.  Helpful, possibly.<P><B>I'd like to hear/see more on the CVT tranny if you could provide a few links.  Maybe we can be better help if we can have an image and some info to work with.</B><P>Just found this out tonight.  I think we run the 94 series. <A HREF="http://www.hoffcocomet.com/comet/torqueindex.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hoffcocomet.com/comet/torqueindex.htm</A> <P>Ok so here is what happened at the meeting tonight.  A few people showed up with a little bit of information but not really with any solid ideas.  One thing that someone found was a "forward/reverse gearbox" that just happened to be made by Comet also and already set up for the CVT. <A HREF="http://www.hoffcocomet.com/comet/specialindex.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hoffcocomet.com/comet/specialindex.htm</A>  In order to get the reduction we need we would run the thing in reverse.  Also there was talk about taking out the forward gear (our reverse).  I think it weighed about 15 lbs and is actually quite small.  So I bring up my idea about the gearhead.  They ask what my unit is made for and I say industrial applications for driving conveyor belts and such.  Their response "Oh that's using electric motors they won't hold up to the torque spikes."  I respond by asking if we had any numbers yet on what our predicted torque spikes are.  Of course they didn't even though a couple of us have been after them for this for quite some time.  So I started explaining an idea I had to put it on a rotating mount that use some type of shock absorber to absorb the torque spike.  To this I got the response "no that won't work you will lose all of your power."  At this point I stopped trying and never got out anything about a homemade motorcycle trans because as you can see they aren't to interested in actually engineering this thing.  The leader of our team did see the specks that I brought about the gear head and when he saw an efficiency rating of 90% he started to get interested but since he was the one that lame statement I just replied "no it won't work we will lose all of our power.  Right now I am going to try to figure out some numbers for the torque we need to handle.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jefrysuko||11-01-2001||02:31 AM||||Here are a few other sites<P>List of other teams websites <A HREF="http://www.sae.org/students/mbusa.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sae.org/students/mbusa.htm</A> <P>Our Website <A HREF="http://eng-council.cen.uiuc.edu/soc/sae/baja/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://eng-council.cen.uiuc.edu/soc/sae/baja/index.html</A> <P>This is a car from two years ago<BR> <IMG SRC="http://eng-council.cen.uiuc.edu/soc/sae/baja/photos/2000/baja%20sponsorshot.jpg"> <P>And this is one from three years back<BR> <IMG SRC="http://eng-council.cen.uiuc.edu/soc/sae/baja/photos/s_w99-11.jpg"> <P>That is it for now.  I need to get to bed so that I can function tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Jefrysuko||11-02-2001||01:39 AM||||Ok guy's just thought that I would give a quick update for those that might be following.  We had a workday today and I spent some time this morning getting some more info on gearheads.  I found a company that can make one with various shaft lengths etc.  This is what it looks like.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.parkergearhead.com/pk_images/inline.gif"> <BR>The shaft you see is the output and the input is on the other side which we would probably want to make into a keyed input coupling or a shaft.  I just sent AutoXdbdragSE a PM and am going to get that trans from him.  By the way you need to shorten your name  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Anyway, this weekend I am going to work on making a quick model in pro-e of my proposed system and I will try and get a picture of that for here.  The car is also coming along.  I cut the tubing for the a-arms and helped a little with the tube fitting for the nose. If all goes well we should have the nose and a-arms done by next week.<P>Nashco, once I get that trans and have a chance to break into it I might have some questions for you about their operation and how I can make it do what I want it to.||||reg||1
